      "text": "To determine the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\), we first need to find the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n\\]\n\nNext, we use the formula for the sum of the divisors of a number. If a number \\(n\\) has the prime factorization \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\), then the sum of the divisors of \\(n\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2)\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31\n\\]\n\\[\n1 + 5 + 25 = 31\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the distinct prime factors of \\(961\\). Notice that:\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\n\nThe prime factorization of \\(961\\) is \\(31^2\\), which means it has only one distinct prime factor, \\(31\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
