      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to perform the following steps:\n\n1. Find the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n2. Calculate the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\).\n3. Determine the distinct prime factors of the sum of the divisors.\n\n### Step 1: Prime Factorization of \\(400\\)\n\nWe can start by finding the prime factorization of \\(400\\).\n\\[400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\]\n\n### Step 2: Sum of the Divisors\n\nThe formula for the sum of the divisors of a number \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \\cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\cdots + p_k^{e_k})\n\\]\n\nFor \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\), the sum of the divisors is:\n\\[\n\\sigma(400) = (1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) = (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) = 31 \\times 31 = 961\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Prime Factorization of the Sum of the Divisors\n\nNow we need to find the prime factorization of \\(961\\).\n\\[\n961 = 31^2\n\\]\n\nThe number \\(961\\) has only one distinct prime factor, which is \\(31\\).\n\nThus, the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\) has \\(\\boxed{1}\\) distinct prime factor.\n\nWe can verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of our result.\n```python\nimport sympy\n\n# Step 1: Prime factorization of 400\nn = 400\nprime_factors = sympy.factorint(n)\nprint((n, prime_factors))\n```",
